    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "In Utah, asphalt shingles typically cost less upfront (around $4.50 to $8.00 per sq. ft. installed) and last 20 to 30 years, while metal roofing costs roughly 1.5 to 2 times more initially (around $8.00 to $16.00 per sq. ft. installed) and lasts 40 to 70+ years. Asphalt suits tighter budgets or shorter ownership plans, whereas metal handles heavy snow, high-altitude UV, and temperature swings better over the long run.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "asphalt shingles typically cost less upfront (around $4.50 to $8.00 per sq. ft. installed) and last 20 to 30 years, while metal roofing costs roughly 1.5 to 2 times more initially (around $8.00 to $16.00 per sq. ft. installed) and lasts 40 to 70+ years"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Comparison for Utah Homes"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Upfront vs. Long-Term Cost: Asphalt shingles have a much lower initial price tag. Metal costs more upfront, though its longer lifespan can make the annual cost comparable if you stay in the home long enough.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Snow & Wind: Metal sheds snow smoothly\u2014helping reduce ice damming\u2014and generally offers stronger wind and impact resistance. Snow guards are often added on metal roofs over walkways, entries, or driveways.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Sun & Temperature Swings: Utah's intense high-altitude UV and big day-to-night temperature shifts can dry out, crack, or loosen granules on asphalt shingles over time. Metal resists UV degradation and reflects more solar heat.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              3,
              4,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Maintenance & Noise: Metal generally needs minimal maintenance once installed, while shingles may need periodic repairs or debris clearing. Properly insulated/decked metal roofs are quiet, though heavy rain or hail can still be more noticeable than on thick shingles.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "For a deeper local breakdown of costs and lifespans, see King Roofing or Homer Roofing .",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
